Rust-Velvet

Ingredients: K-R&B + REBEL, ANGRY & AGGRESSIVE + NEURAL DSP ARCHETYPE GOJIRA X + 1950s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The sound was reverse-engineered from a corrupted cassette found in a time capsule, where the tape’s natural degradation warped 1950s crooning into a mangled, distorted howl.

Rust-Velvet is the sonic equivalent of a tuxedo being shredded by a industrial woodchipper. It juxtaposes the slick, pristine vocal precision of K-R&B with the weaponized, detuned aggression of Gojira-inspired high-gain guitar chains. The 1950s influence manifests as ghost-like, high-pass-filtered piano and brass stabs that seem to float, untethered, above a cavernous, sub-bass-heavy floor.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 70–90 BPM, emphasizing a "dragged" swing that feels like a stuttering heartbeat.
*   Harmonic Palette: Diminished 7th chords typical of 50s doo-wop, but re-voiced through dissonant, chugging drop-tuned guitar intervals.
*   Production Signatures: 
    1. Tape-Rot Filtering: Running all dry signals through a chain that mimics the specific wobbling flutter of degraded 1950s magnetic tape.
    2. Fractured Vocals: Utilizing "Hard-Tuning" (extreme auto-tune) on aggressive, shouting vocal deliveries to create a synthetic, robotic cruelty.
    3. The Void-Chamber: Reverb tails that are processed via AI spectral-gate to suddenly disappear at the end of every phrase.
*   Dynamics: A constant, simmering pressure that explodes into walls of abrasive white-noise guitar, only to snap back into a lonely, clean piano motif.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the windowless basements of Seoul’s underground electronics district, Rust-Velvet is the aesthetic of "Digital Disenchantment." Its practitioners—mostly former conservatory students and gear-hackers—wear bespoke 1950s evening wear stained with soldering lead and engine oil. The primary ritual involves the "Ceremony of the Coil," where the band plays live through a custom-built, electromagnetic interference machine that actively corrupts their own instruments in real-time. It is the music of the disillusioned, those who find the modern world too polished and seek to break it with the ghosts of the past.

Instruments: Neural DSP Archetype Gojira X (for processing everything from vocals to kicks), 1950s Upright Piano (heavily sampled and bit-crushed), sub-bass synthesizers, and vintage ribbon microphones recording in wind tunnels.

For Fans Of: The Weeknd (twisted), Gojira (deconstructed), Burial (if he grew up on Buddy Holly), and Arca.

